WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:02.601
-Our next guest
is Tony-nominated

00:00:02.601 --> 00:00:04.070
and Grammy Award-winning actor.

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:05.738
He's currently
starring on Broadway

00:00:05.738 --> 00:00:07.039
in "Gutenberg! The Musical!",

00:00:07.039 --> 00:00:09.108
now playing at
the James Earl Jones Theatre.

00:00:09.108 --> 00:00:10.276
Please welcome back
to the show,

00:00:10.276 --> 00:00:12.278
our friend Andrew Rannells,
everybody.

00:00:12.278 --> 00:00:14.280
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:30.796 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:30.796 --> 00:00:33.366
Welcome back!
-Hi!

00:00:33.366 --> 00:00:35.702
It feels like it's been
a very long time

00:00:35.702 --> 00:00:37.103
since I've been here.
Hi, Syd.

00:00:37.103 --> 00:00:40.807
-I think, in person, 2019.
So it has been a bit of time.

00:00:40.807 --> 00:00:42.308
-There's been --
Yes, I saw photos...

00:00:42.308 --> 00:00:43.510
-Yeah.
-...of this very thing.

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:44.977
-And we looked great.
-We did look great.

00:00:44.977 --> 00:00:46.879
We look the same.
-And we look the same now.

00:00:46.879 --> 00:00:48.547
-Nothing has changed.

00:00:48.547 --> 00:00:51.417
-I had your friend and co-star
Josh Gad on the show.

00:00:51.417 --> 00:00:52.886
You seem upset
that he was here.

00:00:52.886 --> 00:00:54.687
-No, no, no, no, I just kind
of thought this was my thing.

00:00:54.687 --> 00:00:55.755
[ Laughter ]

00:00:55.755 --> 00:00:58.124
And, you know,
he's, like, an L.A. guy,

00:00:58.124 --> 00:00:59.625
and I was like,
"This is sort of my show."

00:00:59.625 --> 00:01:01.761
-Oh, yeah. Like,
you're kind of a Seth guest.

00:01:01.761 --> 00:01:02.828
And he's kind of like
an out-there guest.

00:01:02.828 --> 00:01:04.597
-Somebody. Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:01:04.597 --> 00:01:05.899
It was --
-But it was fine.

00:01:05.899 --> 00:01:07.467
That was nice of you
to be so nice to him.

00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:09.602
-Yes.
-I appreciate that.

00:01:09.602 --> 00:01:11.070
He's very fragile.
-Yeah.

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:12.972
And L.A. people, like,
they scare easily.

00:01:12.972 --> 00:01:14.941
-Oh, my God.
They're super scared.

00:01:14.941 --> 00:01:17.076
But, yeah, I really did
drag him --

00:01:17.076 --> 00:01:19.278
Because that was his first time
doing your show,

00:01:19.278 --> 00:01:21.948
and I was like,
"Oh, I've done it like 10,"

00:01:21.948 --> 00:01:23.983
which I don't think is true,
but it's probably close.

00:01:23.983 --> 00:01:25.985
-It's pretty close.
You'd round it up to 10.

00:01:25.985 --> 00:01:27.953
-Yeah. Yeah.
-He was talking about --

00:01:27.953 --> 00:01:29.622
And we were
talking about Broadway

00:01:29.622 --> 00:01:31.057
and tiny dressing rooms.

00:01:31.057 --> 00:01:32.258
-Yes, we do have
tiny dressing rooms.

00:01:32.258 --> 00:01:33.692
-The proximity
of dressing rooms.

00:01:33.692 --> 00:01:34.894
-We are right
next to each other.

00:01:34.894 --> 00:01:37.030
You would think maybe
we would have gotten

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:38.764
a little bit farther apart,
but, no, that's not the case.

00:01:38.764 --> 00:01:40.099
-Because the entire show
is the two of you

00:01:40.099 --> 00:01:41.267
on stage together.
-It's just the two of us.

00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:43.803
We have a three-person band
on stage with us,

00:01:43.803 --> 00:01:45.471
and just Josh and I.

00:01:45.471 --> 00:01:47.706
So we're in this
little space together.

00:01:47.706 --> 00:01:49.642
-He was talking about
your warm-up before the show.

00:01:49.642 --> 00:01:51.277
Do you warm up
in the same way or --

00:01:51.277 --> 00:01:53.746
-We do not.
No, Josh, um...

00:01:53.746 --> 00:01:55.048
Josh's vocal warm-up

00:01:55.048 --> 00:01:57.783
is, like, mostly like '80s,
like, power ballads.

00:01:57.783 --> 00:02:00.386
-Yeah.
-And I stick to more sort of

00:02:00.386 --> 00:02:04.123
traditional, like, musical
theater things to warm up to.

00:02:04.123 --> 00:02:07.393
But recently,
it's been very strange.

00:02:07.393 --> 00:02:10.263
Our dresser, Alex Bartlett,
and I have been very confused.

00:02:10.263 --> 00:02:12.031
Sometimes during intermission,
Josh will go into

00:02:12.031 --> 00:02:15.201
his dressing room and listen to,
like, acoustic versions of

00:02:15.201 --> 00:02:19.972
"Everybody Hurts" or "Creep"
or, like --

00:02:19.972 --> 00:02:21.907
Like, it's really --
it gets real dark,

00:02:21.907 --> 00:02:23.742
and we're like, "Joshy..."
-And that's a very counter

00:02:23.742 --> 00:02:25.011
energy to the play.
-No, yeah.

00:02:25.011 --> 00:02:27.746
This show is like --
it's, like, all high energy.

00:02:27.746 --> 00:02:29.515
And Josh is in there like,

00:02:29.515 --> 00:02:31.618
I guess,
slowly rocking in the dark.

00:02:31.618 --> 00:02:33.553
I don't know what he's doing.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:33.553 --> 00:02:34.854
But I'm concerned.
-They are long days

00:02:34.854 --> 00:02:36.188
when you have double shows.

00:02:36.188 --> 00:02:38.258
He was saying that he will ta--
he will lie on a --

00:02:38.258 --> 00:02:39.425
He will take a nap on a cot

00:02:39.425 --> 00:02:41.794
that you have described
as his FEMA cot.

00:02:41.794 --> 00:02:43.696
-Well, it is
a little jarring.

00:02:43.696 --> 00:02:46.132
Usually --
Yeah, I mean, I'm all for naps.

00:02:46.132 --> 00:02:47.300
Don't get me wrong.

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:49.836
But he does sleep
like sort of an infant.

00:02:49.836 --> 00:02:52.338
And he sleeps wherever
and whenever he can.

00:02:52.338 --> 00:02:54.840
So they have, like,
a full rollaway bed

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:57.243
that he pulls into his room
from the hallway

00:02:57.243 --> 00:02:58.711
and just lays down and, I guess,

00:02:58.711 --> 00:03:00.913
cries himself to sleep.
I don't know.

00:03:00.913 --> 00:03:03.615
-Due to his acoustic --
his acoustic sad-song mix.

00:03:03.615 --> 00:03:06.085
-Yeah, but he takes --
he takes a lot of naps.

00:03:06.085 --> 00:03:07.353
I mean, naps are important.

00:03:07.353 --> 00:03:08.688
Broadway is hard.
-Broadway is hard.

00:03:08.688 --> 00:03:11.591
-Eight shows. You know,
it's a very active show.

00:03:11.591 --> 00:03:13.058
It's just the two of us.
-Just the two of you.

00:03:13.058 --> 00:03:15.661
Now, one nice thing, though,
is, the audience

00:03:15.661 --> 00:03:17.996
doesn't play a role, but there's
no fourth wall in the show.

00:03:17.996 --> 00:03:19.531
So you get to talk
to the audience.

00:03:19.531 --> 00:03:21.834
-Which is a good thing
and a bad thing, as you know.

00:03:21.834 --> 00:03:24.737
Like, opening the door
to the audience of, like,

00:03:24.737 --> 00:03:26.539
there's sort of a false sense
of, like,

00:03:26.539 --> 00:03:28.608
"Do we participate?"
and the answer is no.

00:03:28.608 --> 00:03:29.608
[ Laughter ]

00:03:29.608 --> 00:03:31.543
-They're there to be spoken to.

00:03:31.543 --> 00:03:33.745
-You do not get to participate.

00:03:33.745 --> 00:03:37.583
So -- Well, you know,
we are introducing ourselves,

00:03:37.583 --> 00:03:39.752
and we're explaining the show
and -- and, you know,

00:03:39.752 --> 00:03:42.522
most of the time,
most audiences are fantastic.

00:03:42.522 --> 00:03:44.890
And then some audiences,
we get some -- like, some jokers

00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:47.894
who are like, "Hey, Olaf!"

00:03:47.894 --> 00:03:50.696
You know,
and they do some like...

00:03:50.696 --> 00:03:52.532
"That's not what
we're doing, sir."

00:03:52.532 --> 00:03:54.934
-And do you just basically
have the -- have the permission

00:03:54.934 --> 00:03:56.435
to say that to them?

00:03:56.435 --> 00:03:57.937
-Kind of. Yeah.
In this show.

00:03:57.937 --> 00:04:00.306
I mean, I --

00:04:00.306 --> 00:04:03.142
Yes, I have, like,
a little bit more --

00:04:03.142 --> 00:04:04.443
What's the best way to say this?

00:04:04.443 --> 00:04:05.711
A tiny, more experience

00:04:05.711 --> 00:04:08.347
in front of a live audience
than Josh does.

00:04:08.347 --> 00:04:09.982
This is Josh's first time back.

00:04:09.982 --> 00:04:11.517
I'm talking about him
like he's dead.

00:04:11.517 --> 00:04:13.719
But he's -- it's his first time
back to Broadway

00:04:13.719 --> 00:04:15.287
since "The Book of Mormon."
-Yeah.

00:04:15.287 --> 00:04:17.790
-Whoo!
-And -- thank you.

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:21.093
And, Josh --

00:04:21.093 --> 00:04:22.962
You know,
the general rule of improv,

00:04:22.962 --> 00:04:25.364
Seth, is, "Yes, and..."
-"Yes, and..."

00:04:25.364 --> 00:04:26.632
-Correct?
-Whatever your partner says,

00:04:26.632 --> 00:04:27.866
you got to roll with it.

00:04:27.866 --> 00:04:30.869
-So, you know,
inevitably things will go wrong

00:04:30.869 --> 00:04:33.072
in a Broadway show,
and in this show,

00:04:33.072 --> 00:04:35.374
we sort of differentiate
all these different characters

00:04:35.374 --> 00:04:37.976
we play by wearing these hats

00:04:37.976 --> 00:04:39.312
with the characters' names
on them,

00:04:39.312 --> 00:04:42.281
and, usually,
everything goes fine.

00:04:42.281 --> 00:04:43.616
And sometimes there's
a little --

00:04:43.616 --> 00:04:44.984
you know,
there can be a little kerfuffle

00:04:44.984 --> 00:04:46.419
or, you know, it's a new show.

00:04:46.419 --> 00:04:48.421
We were in previews, so there
are some changes and stuff.

00:04:48.421 --> 00:04:50.389
So, one night,
we were doing the show,

00:04:50.389 --> 00:04:53.526
and I skipped like four pages.

00:04:53.526 --> 00:04:55.727
[ Laughter ]

00:04:55.727 --> 00:04:57.830
Like, four full pages.

00:04:57.830 --> 00:05:00.165
And -- But in my mind,
I was thinking about it,

00:05:00.165 --> 00:05:04.937
and I was like, "Well, I could
try to go back, but why?"

00:05:04.937 --> 00:05:07.607
And I was like, "It's probably
for the better for everyone,

00:05:07.607 --> 00:05:10.042
for the crew.
Everyone wants to go home."

00:05:10.042 --> 00:05:14.013
So I just kind of kept going
and just was like,

00:05:14.013 --> 00:05:15.547
"We'll just
skip the four pages."

00:05:15.547 --> 00:05:17.049
-And were you making
eye contact with him

00:05:17.049 --> 00:05:19.085
as if to be like,
"I acknowledge I missed it,

00:05:19.085 --> 00:05:20.753
but let's keep going"?
-Well, that's on me.

00:05:20.753 --> 00:05:22.288
-Okay.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:22.288 --> 00:05:24.490
Perhaps I didn't
do that clearly enough,

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:28.027
but his improv, to me, was,

00:05:28.027 --> 00:05:29.562
"You don't know
where the [bleep] you are."

00:05:29.562 --> 00:05:31.297
[ Laughter ]

00:05:31.297 --> 00:05:32.632
-He said that on stage
for the audience to hear.

00:05:32.632 --> 00:05:33.766
-Yeah.

00:05:33.766 --> 00:05:35.368
-Yeah, that's not good improv
at all.

00:05:35.368 --> 00:05:40.139
-No. And to which I replied,
"No, I don't."

00:05:40.139 --> 00:05:41.307
And then we got back on track.

00:05:41.307 --> 00:05:43.008
But it was not --
-That's not kind.

00:05:43.008 --> 00:05:44.576
-It was, "No, but..."
-Did he make you go back,

00:05:44.576 --> 00:05:46.379
or did you keep going?
-Oh, I kept going.

00:05:46.379 --> 00:05:47.780
-Yeah, yeah.
You can't then --

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:49.515
-I punished them all.
-You know why you can't?

00:05:49.515 --> 00:05:51.550
Because if you then went back,

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:54.287
the audience would see
exactly how much you'd missed

00:05:54.287 --> 00:05:55.754
when you got back to the place
you were at.

00:05:55.754 --> 00:05:59.658
-And I'll be honest,
I have no clue what I missed.

00:05:59.658 --> 00:06:00.960
And I really did.
I went backstage,

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:02.595
and I was like,
"I know I missed a lot."

00:06:02.595 --> 00:06:03.729
And they were like,
"It's four pages."

00:06:03.729 --> 00:06:05.097
I was like, "Oh, okay.
Well..."

00:06:05.097 --> 00:06:07.133
-You also have a very
cool thing in the show.

00:06:07.133 --> 00:06:08.734
There is a cameo every night.

00:06:08.734 --> 00:06:12.004
-Oh, yes. Yes, we do.
We have a special guest cameo.

00:06:12.004 --> 00:06:14.006
-And do you -- how far
in advance do you know

00:06:14.006 --> 00:06:15.474
who the cameo will be?

00:06:15.474 --> 00:06:17.342
-Sometimes the day of.
-Gotcha.

00:06:17.342 --> 00:06:19.011
-You know, but we do rehearse
with the person

00:06:19.011 --> 00:06:20.112
a little bit before.

00:06:20.112 --> 00:06:22.882
So we actually
just had your friend

00:06:22.882 --> 00:06:25.984
and also fellow Omahan
Amber Ruffin.

00:06:25.984 --> 00:06:28.487
-Amber Ruffin, the great
Amber Ruffin, in the show.

00:06:28.487 --> 00:06:30.990
-Who killed it.
Was hilarious.

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:33.526
-You also -- the show is called
"Gutenberg!" and you just had --

00:06:33.526 --> 00:06:36.028
-We had Steve Guttenberg.
-You had Steve Guttenberg.

00:06:36.028 --> 00:06:38.697
-Steve Guttenberg, spelled
with two T's, in case

00:06:38.697 --> 00:06:40.899
anyone's keeping track.

00:06:40.899 --> 00:06:43.635
"Gutenberg" is --
Johann is with one,

00:06:43.635 --> 00:06:46.038
Steve is with two.
No, he was fantastic.

00:06:46.038 --> 00:06:50.343
And I -- you know, he was
a huge part of my childhood.

00:06:50.343 --> 00:06:52.578
And he's, like, in so many
movies from that time.

00:06:52.578 --> 00:06:55.213
-That you watch on VHS tape
a thousand times each.

00:06:55.213 --> 00:06:57.883
-A thousand times.
Yeah. So that was very exciting.

00:06:57.883 --> 00:06:59.251
-Alex Timbers is the director.
Alex Timbers.

00:06:59.251 --> 00:07:00.653
-Wonderful director.
-Fantastic director.

00:07:00.653 --> 00:07:03.288
-A collaborative director.
-Very collaborative, yes.

00:07:03.288 --> 00:07:04.823
-But yet you've worked
with directors in the past --

00:07:04.823 --> 00:07:06.592
Have you ever received notes
from directors that you were

00:07:06.592 --> 00:07:10.530
maybe trying to be constructive
but rang a little...?

00:07:10.530 --> 00:07:13.298
-Yeah, early on,
I think sometimes they get --

00:07:13.298 --> 00:07:14.666
Well,
what am I saying, early on?

00:07:14.666 --> 00:07:17.136
Sometimes still today.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:17.136 --> 00:07:18.804
...get impatient,
and they want to say --

00:07:18.804 --> 00:07:19.938
And I'm all for it.

00:07:19.938 --> 00:07:22.475
Like, if you want it
faster or louder...

00:07:22.475 --> 00:07:23.743
-Yeah.
-...just say faster or louder.

00:07:23.743 --> 00:07:26.078
'Cause sometimes people
can get into like,

00:07:26.078 --> 00:07:28.614
"Well, I wonder if there
should be more immediacy."

00:07:28.614 --> 00:07:30.749
You're like, "You mean faster."
[ Laughter ]

00:07:30.749 --> 00:07:32.251
"You want it faster?
That's great."

00:07:32.251 --> 00:07:34.119
So I like to cut to the chase.
Don't get me wrong.

00:07:34.119 --> 00:07:37.156
-Right.
-But I did have a director --

00:07:37.156 --> 00:07:38.657
He gave me --
I used to keep them

00:07:38.657 --> 00:07:41.327
'cause he would write his notes
down and hand them out.

00:07:41.327 --> 00:07:44.396
My favorites were
"Quit acting with your hair."

00:07:44.396 --> 00:07:48.067
[ Laughter ]
That's a good one.

00:07:48.067 --> 00:07:50.102
"Charming is not a choice."

00:07:50.102 --> 00:07:51.237
[ Laughter ]

00:07:51.237 --> 00:07:53.338
I beg to differ.

00:07:53.338 --> 00:07:55.908
I could choose to be a dick
right now, but I'm not.

00:07:55.908 --> 00:07:58.777
And, um -- oh, the other
good one was,

00:07:58.777 --> 00:08:00.946
"Andrew,
you are not James Dean,"

00:08:00.946 --> 00:08:03.182
which I don't really know
how that applied to the show

00:08:03.182 --> 00:08:06.919
we were doing, but I think maybe
he was just tired or confused.

00:08:06.919 --> 00:08:08.721
-He was like, "I got to give him
a note on today."

00:08:08.721 --> 00:08:10.122
-Something.
-"Let him know who he's not."

00:08:10.122 --> 00:08:12.124
-"You're not James Dean." Okay.
-Yeah, I have to say --

00:08:12.124 --> 00:08:13.759
Can I say something?
I think "Book of Mormon"

00:08:13.759 --> 00:08:15.761
would've sucked with James Dean.
-Thank you.

00:08:15.761 --> 00:08:16.929
-Yeah.
-He would've just, like,

00:08:16.929 --> 00:08:19.298
mumbled his way through it.
-Way too broody.

00:08:19.298 --> 00:08:21.066
-Not fun at all.
-Really -- Yeah.

00:08:21.066 --> 00:08:22.501
So I'm glad I'm not James Dean.
-Yeah.

00:08:22.501 --> 00:08:24.336
-It worked out.
-I'm even happier.

00:08:24.336 --> 00:08:25.938
-But I'm still acting
with my hair, so take that.

00:08:25.938 --> 00:08:28.107
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
And can I say something?

00:08:28.107 --> 00:08:29.508
You're very charming
when you do it.

00:08:29.508 --> 00:08:31.076
Yeah, and it's a choice.
-Seth!

00:08:31.076 --> 00:08:32.911
-It's a choice I care for.
-Come on.

00:08:32.911 --> 00:08:34.714
-You guys,
that's Andrew Rannells.

00:08:34.714 --> 00:08:37.015
"Gutenberg! The Musical!"
is playing now

00:08:37.015 --> 00:08:39.184
at the James Earl Jones Theatre.
Go see it, and stick around.

00:08:39.184 --> 00:08:41.888
Be right back with Jesmyn Ward.

